include g474.tdc
diagram hsw-slow "Thread → thread with USE_SWITCH=y, slow mode (78 %): four SysTick reprograms in one switch"
letter "H-sw slow"
new
num value "about 3169 cycles"
num share "77.8 %"
num path "k_sem, giver (prio 2) → taker (prio 1)"
num arch "USE_SWITCH=y (no PendSV)"
what "The same path as card H-sw fast, but here the giver's slice timeout is the first timeout. Each of the two slice resets aborts it (a reprogram for the next timeout) and adds a new one (a reprogram again). So one switch programs SysTick four times, against two with PendSV. The second reset is for the same thread and the same slice. The dashed cursor shows where the fast mode ends (card H-sw fast)."
evidence "The slow mode is in the 32-cycle bins 3136 (24.0 %) and 3168 (53.8 %). Its mean is about 3169 (from the mean of the histogram)."
evidence "The four reprograms together: about 1389 cycles, measured as the mode distance (1780 / 3169); the split into four is an estimate. With PendSV: 680 (two reprograms). The run of 2026-10-25 gave 1289 (ws-safety) and 1333 (ws-up)."
evidence "Prediction written before the run of 2026-10-25 (later.md L18): about 1300 cycles. Mutex: about 1376."
evidence "An ISR wake does not reach do_swap(), so it keeps one reprogram with USE_SWITCH=y (card E-sw slow)."
evidence "Kernel clock drift: 1190 → 1752 ppm (sem): more reprograms lose more cycles (card I)."
src "thread-switch/20261029T024719Z"

axis "cycles after the give call" -100..3300

taker pending -100.. "blocked in k_sem_take()" : The taker waits in k_sem_take() with K_FOREVER.

at -100
giver 100 : The giver was woken by the stimulus ISR.
@give
@fast ref "fast mode" at give+1780
giver 300 "give" {basepri} : The giver gives the semaphore. The taker has a higher priority, so update_cache() selects it as the next thread.
giver 310? "reset 1" {basepri} : update_cache() resets the slice for the taker.
@r0 anchor
giver 347 "reprogram" {basepri} : Abort of the giver's slice timeout. It was the first timeout, so the driver programs SysTick for the next one.
giver 347 "reprogram" {basepri} : Add of the taker's slice timeout. It is the first timeout again, so the driver programs SysTick again.
@r1 anchor
giver 310? "reset 2" {basepri} : do_swap() calls z_time_slice_reset(new_thread) again (kernel/include/kswap.h:130).
@r2 anchor
giver 347 "reprogram" {basepri} : Abort of the taker's slice timeout, which reset 1 just added. It is the first timeout, so SysTick is programmed again.
giver 348 "reprogram" {basepri} : Add of the taker's slice timeout again: the fourth reprogram of this switch.
@r3 anchor
giver 773 "arch_switch()" {basepri} : Inline in z_swap(), in thread mode: save the giver, restore the taker. No exception.
taker 87 : The taker returns from k_sem_take() and reads TIM2->CNT.
@runs "taker runs"
giver pending runs..3300 "preempted" : The giver is ready, but the taker has the higher priority.

span give..runs "about 3169, measured" !
span r0..r1 "2 reprograms"
span r2..r3 "2 reprograms"
